THE EDIT THAT IS NOT A REMOVAL, AND IT CARRIED NO MARKER.  Every modification
this document had described until now was a deletion, a rename, a guard or an
`#include` spelling.  There is one that is none of those, it is in XQA rather
than here, and it went in after the strip without a `// PIE:` marker and
without any count being re-run:

    xqa/mha.cuh:1455    result.fill(AccType{});
    upstream mha.cu     result.fill(AccType{0, 0});

`2af552a24`, in `smemFp16ArraySum`.  `AccType` is
`mha::conditional_t<useFp32Acc, float2, InputElem2>`; for the bf16 lattice
`INPUT_ELEM2` is `__nv_bfloat162` (`xqa/defines.h:41`), which
`shim/cuda_bf16.h:262` aliases to the prelude's `device::bf16x2` --
`struct { bf16 x; bf16 y; }` whose element type declares its constructors
`explicit`, so a braced `{0, 0}` copy-initialising each member cannot see one.
NVIDIA's `__nv_bfloat162` takes it; ours refuses it.  `AccType{}`
value-initialises and is zero for the `float2` arm too.  The cause is OUR shim,
not upstream, which is what makes this a modification to record rather than a
bug to report upstream.

It costs 4 bytes and no lines, and it is the reason the XQA byte total in
`NOTICE` read 265,143 against a tree of 265,139: a line count cannot see it, a
byte count can, and neither was re-run.  It is step 3 work on a rebase -- put
`AccType{0, 0}` back and the file is upstream's, and it will not compile here
until it is changed again.

IT CARRIES NO MARKER, AND THAT IS A DECISION RATHER THAN AN OMISSION.  A
marker is a line of its own -- step 1 of the recipe above strips whole marker
lines, so a trailing one on the code line could not be removed without taking
the code with it -- and `mha.cuh`'s header note pins the arithmetic that makes
its citations readable: TWO insertions, both above the cited lines, so upstream
`:409` is `:435` here and `:2757` is `:2783`.  A third insertion at 1454 shifts
every citation below it and silently falsifies both of those worked examples.
The cost of the marker is larger than the cost of the record.

So the record is here, and `mha.cuh`'s own header says so at the top of the
file where a rebaser will read it before touching anything.  What a strip must
not do is produce a file that claims to be upstream's and is not; naming the
line in two places it will be read is what prevents that.

THE SECOND EDIT IS UPSTREAM'S BUG RATHER THAN OUR SHIM'S, AND IT IS THE ONLY
ONE OF THESE THAT A ROOT COULD NOT COMPILE WITHOUT:

    attention/mla.cuh:762    o_smem.stmatrix_m8n8x4(o_smem_offset_w, o_frag_f16);
    upstream mla.cuh:762     o_smem.template stmatrix_m8n8x4(o_smem_offset_w, o_frag_f16);

`smem_t::stmatrix_m8n8x4` (`permuted_smem.cuh:148`) is not a template.  The
`template` disambiguator asserts that the name which follows IS one, so the
compiler demands the argument list that a non-template cannot take, and NVRTC
says *"argument list for template ... is missing"*.  Upstream's own TEN other
call sites -- `mla.cuh:531` and nine in `prefill.cuh`, from `:892` to `:1900`
-- all spell it without the keyword; 762 is the only one that does not.  This
is not our shim making an upstream line unbuildable, which is what the XQA
edit above was; it is a line that no compiler accepts, and reporting it
upstream is the right disposition.

IT WAS INVISIBLE UNTIL THE ARCH REACHED IT.  The line sits under
`#ifdef FLASHINFER_STMATRIX_M8N8X4_ENABLED`, which `mma.cuh:38` defines only
for `__CUDA_ARCH__ >= 900`.  Every arch below that compiles the `#else` branch
and never parses the faulty one, so the bug ships dead and stays dead until
something targets Hopper or newer.  `every_instantiation_compiles` is what
reached it: `attn/attention_mla_fa2` was 1 of 127 roots failing, and the whole
kernel was unreachable at run time on this machine because a JIT root that
does not compile is a kernel that does not exist.

IT CARRIES NO MARKER FOR THE SAME ARITHMETIC AS THE FIRST, AND THE CITATIONS
