HOST C++ IS GONE, AND THAT IS WHY THIS TABLE LOST FOUR ROWS.  The move
carried 28 files.  Four of them contained no device code at all -- not one
`__global__` body, not one `__device__` function -- and they are deleted:

    attention/scheduler.cuh    1     13    1840     87507
    exception.h                1      9     115      4194
    allocator.h                1      7     111      4038
    attention/heap.h           1      6      72      2108

That is 2,138 lines and 97,847 bytes of pure host C++ that NVRTC was being
handed and could never have compiled.  Every one of them was guarded in its
ENTIRETY, so NVRTC saw four empty files; the guards were doing the work a
deletion does, at the cost of shipping the text.  Each has a Rust owner that
predates this deletion:

    attention/scheduler.cuh    src/plan/{decode,prefill,mla,sm90}.rs
    exception.h                src/plan/error.rs
    allocator.h                src/plan/alloc.rs
    attention/heap.h           src/plan/heap.rs

The measurement that made this safe, and it was the one worth running:
`scheduler.cuh` is `#include`d by NOTHING in this tree, and `allocator.h`
and `heap.h` are `#include`d only by `scheduler.cuh`.  Walking the four
compilation roots -- `attn/fa2.cuh`, `attn/attention_mla_fa2.cuh`,
`attn/attention_xqa_mha.cuh`, `cascade/merge_states.cuh` -- reached 40 of
the 43 internalised files, and the three it missed were exactly those.  They
were unreachable weight, not a dependency.

`exception.h` is the fourth and the only one that was reachable, from
`page.cuh` and from `utils.cuh`.  Deleting it therefore deleted two
`#include` lines, which is step 3 above and was, at the time, the only edit
in this tree that a strip could not undo.  It is safe for a reason that is
easy to state and worth stating: because `exception.h` was guarded in its
entirety, `FLASHINFER_CHECK` and `FLASHINFER_ERROR` were ALREADY undefined in
every NVRTC translation unit here.  24 expansions survived it, inside
`#define` bodies NVRTC never expands or inside host launchers NVRTC never
reaches.  Removing the launchers took the second kind with them: 13
expansions remain and every one is a `#define` continuation line.  The
deletion changed what NVRTC sees by nothing at all, and still does.

`scheduler.cuh`'s three `__global__` lines were forward declarations with no
bodies, present only so `cudaOccupancyMaxActiveBlocksPerMultiprocessor` had a
function pointer to ask about.  Under the JIT that query is Rust's, so there
was never a kernel in that file to keep.

AND A FIFTH FILE WENT, FOR THE OPPOSITE REASON.  The four above were deleted
because they are host C++ that NVRTC could never compile.  `fp16.h` is the
other case: 179 lines of pure device text, every one of it `constexpr` and
device-callable, deleted because **no compile has ever reached it.**  It is
Marat Dukhan's software fp32<->fp16 conversion, redistributed by FlashInfer
under MIT, and `attention/prefill.cuh` opened it behind a guard:

    #ifdef FP16_QK_REDUCTION_SUPPORTED
    #include "../fp16.h"
    #endif

Nothing in this workspace defines `FP16_QK_REDUCTION_SUPPORTED`, so the
directive was never taken -- and defining it would not have helped.
`prefill.cuh:4208-4210` selects a `half` QK accumulator only when
`USE_FP16_QK_REDUCTION && is_same_v<DTypeQ, half>`, and PIE's `DTypeQ` is
`bf16` (`attn/fa2.cuh:227-230` states this and says why).  The feature is off
structurally, not by configuration.

It took two shims with it.  `shim/bit` (`std::bit_cast`) and
`shim/boost/math/ccmath/fabs.hpp` (one `constexpr` absolute value)
existed for `fp16.h` and for nothing else, and `shim/README.md` is the
register for their retirement.  Both are marked RETIRED in the list under
"Not upstream, written by PIE" below, which leaves four of its six live.

The measurement, and it is the one that matters here: with `fp16.h` and both
shims deleted, `tests/every_instantiation_compiles.rs` still passes -- every
`__global__` a unit names lowers under NVRTC 13.0, 37s of real compilation.
A file that can be deleted without a compiler noticing was not a dependency.
`prefill.cuh` carries a `// PIE: REMOVED` marker at the include site and its
row above counts it.  The `#ifdef` blocks at `:247` and `:1312` are upstream's
own and are untouched: their `#else` arms are what compiles, and each already
`static_assert`s that a `__half` accumulator needs the flag, so the diagnostic
a future asker gets names the feature rather than a missing file.

