These six sat here for a provenance reason -- they exist BECAUSE FlashInfer
asks for them, so they were filed under FlashInfer.  Two are marked RETIRED:
`fp16.h` was their only asker and it is deleted, so four remain.  The rows
stay because this list records what was MOVED, and both were.  That is the wrong axis.
What they DO is impersonate a header this compiler does not ship, which is
also what `cuda_fp16.h`, `cuda_bf16.h`, `cuda_fp8.h`, `cuda_fp4.h`,
`cooperative_groups.h` and `cuda/{cmath,pipeline,std/limits}` do, and those
eight sat beside our own device text for the mirror-image reason: our own
text asks for them too.  All fourteen are now in `shim/`, filed by role.  That
directory holds twenty today, and its own README is the register of what
was added and retired since; none of the seven additions is upstream's and
none is counted in the table above.  One of the eight has since been RETIRED
-- `cuda/pipeline`, whose only asker in this tree was the vestigial
`#include` that `permuted_smem.cuh`'s row now counts -- so the sentence above
is the set as it moved, not as it stands.

The move cannot be seen from inside a compile.  NVRTC has no `-I`: it matches
the literal string in the directive against the names in `includeNames[]`, and
those names are unchanged, because for a shim THE NAME IS THE CONTRACT -- a
file called `cuda_fp16.h` is answering for NVIDIA's `cuda_fp16.h` and cannot
be renamed without ceasing to do its job.  nvcc needed one line: a second
`-iquote` at each of the four sites that passed the first.  There are no such
sites left -- nothing in the workspace compiles this device text with nvcc any
more -- so that sentence is history and `shim/README.md` records the same
change at length.

